  const { table, entries } = buildTable([simpleGlyph(), new Uint8Array(0)]);
  expect(entries[1]).toEqual({ offset: table.byteLength, endOffset: table.byteLength });
  const before = table.slice();

  const dropped = pruneCompositeGlyphCycles(table, entries, 2);

  expect(dropped.size).toBe(0);
  expect(bytesOf(table)).toEqual(bytesOf(before));
});
```
Hmm: buildTable with a trailing zero-length glyph: padding = (4 - pos%4)%4 could be nonzero, and then pos advances by padding, so entries[1].offset = padded pos which may be > table.byteLength? Let's see: parts includes padding of length p then empty array. pos increments by padding → so entries[1].offset = pos after padding, and table length = pos (since empty appended). Equal. Good, but if padding pushes pos to e.g. 24 while table length 24 (padding included) → equal → offset == byteLength. Good. Actually simpleGlyph length 18 → pos = 18; padding = 2 → 20; entries[1] = {20,20}; table length = 20. Good.

Test C: strengthened clamp test: last glyph composite with kept comp + self-ref comp with MORE set (so parse would run past table end), plus endOffset beyond byteLength.

